	System monitor program.

Installation Notes:

	The sysmond daemon drops root privileges after initialization, on
its own, with nothing to configure. Its raw ICMP sockets are opened
while still root and keep working afterwards, because a raw socket's
privilege is checked when it is created rather than on every send.

	"make install" also installs a small setuid helper
(sysmon-ping-helper). It is only a fallback, for a kernel that refuses
to send on those sockets once privileges are gone, and the daemon finds
that out from the send itself rather than assuming it.

	Everything the daemon writes goes in one state directory, by
default /var/db/sysmon, created and handed to the unprivileged user at
startup. Nothing else needs making writable.

	You may need ncurses; sysmond speaks SNMP itself and needs no
net-snmp. You should have gcc for
compiling this program. For JSON output support, the daemon can write
monitoring data in JSON format. A web-based configuration manager is
included in the web-ui/ directory.

	You may need ncurses, and should have gcc for compiling this
program.  If you can get me accounts on other platforms of machines (not
root, just accounts), I can port this software to almost everything
UNIX based (I will not port to vms or nt :).

	I claim nothing, this code will erase your hard disk, crash your
network and destroy your users and damage your hardware.  It will
always do this.

New Features (2025):
	- Per-object page interval (pageinterval directive per monitor)
	- Flap detection and dampening (flaptime, minnumfailures config options)
	- SNMP custom up/down messages per object (snmp_up_msg, snmp_down_msg)
	- Per-object custom page messages (pmesg directive per monitor)
	- DNS authoritative answer validation (dns-aa keyword for DNS monitors)
	- DNS recursion control (dns-recursion keyword for DNS monitors)
	- SNMP OID comparison monitoring (snmp-oid-sec for comparing two OIDs)
	- SNMP trap alert monitoring with matched_host support
	- Packet loss monitoring (min_pings, send_pings, packet_loss_threshold)
	- RTT and jitter threshold monitoring (rtt_threshold, jitter_threshold)
	- Wakeup checks for proactive monitoring (wakeup_check, wakeup_retries)
	- Privilege dropping for enhanced security (drop_privileges directive)
	- JSON output mode for machine-readable monitoring data
	- Web UI configuration manager (FastCGI-based, written in Go)
	- IPv6 ICMP support via setuid ping helper
	- Improved error handling and logging

Tested on by author:
	Linux, SunOS, OSF/1, FreeBSD, Solaris, BSDI, NetBSD & NetBSD Alpha,
Solaris x86, HP/UX, Apple OS/10 Server (Rhapsody)

Compiles Under but not tested under by author:
	SCO

